# Trivy suppressions for eks-secure-baseline
#
# POLICY: this file is the ONLY place a finding may be suppressed, every
# entry carries a written justification, and suppressions are per-CHECK-ID.
# The scanner itself is never weakened: `trivy config` still runs with
# --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1, so any finding not listed here
# fails the pipeline. Do not add a blanket severity filter, do not remove
# --exit-code, and do not add an entry without a justification.
#
# Reviewed: 2026-09-19

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0104 — "Security group rule allows egress to multiple public internet
#             addresses" (aws_vpc_security_group_egress_rule.cluster_egress)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCEPTED RISK, not a false positive in general — but not remediable for an
# EKS control plane security group.
#
# The EKS control plane's cross-account ENIs must reach worker node kubelets
# on ephemeral ports and regional AWS service endpoints (STS, ECR, CloudWatch
# Logs, EC2). Those endpoints are not expressible as a stable set of CIDRs or
# prefix lists for this security group, and AWS's own EKS security group
# requirements specify outbound 0.0.0.0/0 for the cluster security group.
# Narrowing it breaks node registration and add-on installation — failures
# that surface only after a full ~15 minute apply.
#
# Compensating controls that make this acceptable:
#   * The SG is attached ONLY to AWS-managed control plane ENIs. No workload
#     traffic transits it.
#   * The SG has NO ingress rules at all: it is egress-only.
#   * Workload egress is separately default-denied by the NetworkPolicies in
#     k8s/hardening/network-policies.yaml, which also block the instance
#     metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254).
#   * Nodes have no public IPs and sit in private subnets behind a NAT gateway.
#
# Scope: this suppresses ONE check id. Every other HIGH/CRITICAL finding in
# infra/ and k8s/ still fails the build.
AWS-0104
